<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 900px; padding: 20px;">

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px;">
        Lesson Planner – Theme # How Much Are the Bananas? – Lesson # 4
    </h2>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Are the Bananas?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create basic informational leaflets for the event. Interactive: Write simple group summaries of chapters read in class.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create an informational leaflet describing farm stand items and prices for customers (e.g., "We have apples for one dollar and bananas for two dollars."). Can write a simple summary of a story (e.g., "The story is about a farm stand. They sell fruits."). Can tell classmates about the farm stand (e.g., "Come to our farm stand for fruit!").</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">
        The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages
    </h3>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various fruits and vegetables found at a farm stand (e.g., mango, pineapple, banana, tomato, lettuce). Students are given a small whiteboard or paper and 3 minutes to write down as many items as they can remember, focusing on correct spelling. The teacher reminds them to pay attention to words with /p/ and /b/ sounds, like "papaya" and "banana."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple sentences describing a farm stand item and its price. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "We have fresh bananas. They are two dollars. How much is a banana? It's cheap!" The teacher articulates /p/ and /b/ clearly and segments words like "banana" (ba-na-na) as they write, drawing attention to syllables and sounds.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we sell at the farm stand?" (Expected: Fruits, vegetables, bread, milk.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do we ask about the cost of something?" (Expected: "How much is...?")</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple informational text (genre: basic advertising copy) about a farm stand, displayed as a 'Farm Stand Price List' on a large poster or projected. The text will include items, descriptions, and prices. Example: "Welcome to Fresh Farm Stand! We have delicious mangoes. They are one dollar. Do you want some pineapple? It is two dollars. We have some fresh lettuce and juicy tomatoes. We don't have any milk today."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to interpret and extract specific information (items, prices, availability) from a simple written text related to a farm stand, preparing them to produce similar texts.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected: A farm stand and its prices.) Students write their answer in their notebooks.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match items from a list (e.g., 'mango', 'pineapple', 'lettuce') to their prices or availability mentioned in the text. They write the item and its detail in pairs (e.g., "mango - one dollar", "milk - not available").</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using "How much is...?", "We have some...", "I don't have any...", polite requests, and target vocabulary (mango, pineapple, banana, tomato, pepper, lettuce, milk, bread, cheese, price, buy, sell, cost, cheap, fresh, ripe, delicious, healthy).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using vocabulary words, prices, or grammar structures (e.g., "We have ___ fresh ___ for two dollars." or "Do you have ___ milk?"). Students write the complete sentences in their notebooks. Example: "How much ___ (is/are) the ___ (banana/bananas)?" or "I would like ___ (some/any) water, please."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Item Description Cards):</b> Each student receives 3-4 picture cards of farm stand items. For each card, they write two simple sentences describing the item and its price, using adjectives like 'fresh', 'ripe', 'delicious' and phrases like "We have...", "It costs...". Example: (Picture of apples) "We have fresh apples. They cost three dollars." (Picture of papaya) "This is a ripe papaya. It costs one dollar." The teacher encourages them to silently segment the syllables of words like "papaya" as they write.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Edit):</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange their written item descriptions from activity 2. Each student reads their partner's sentences and checks for correct vocabulary, grammar (especially "some/any" and "How much...?"), and legibility. They politely suggest one correction or improvement to their partner, focusing on polite expressions (e.g., "Can you help me check this word, please?").</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual or small group (2-3 students) written product: Create a simple informational leaflet for a classroom 'Local Farm Stand' event.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are given a blank sheet of paper or a template to design their own farm stand leaflet. They must include at least 5-7 items from the vocabulary list (e.g., banana, pineapple, bread, cheese, tomato, pepper). For each item, they need to:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write the item's name.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Add a short description (e.g., "fresh," "ripe," "delicious," "healthy").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">State its price using numbers and the dollar sign (e.g., "$2").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use phrases like "We have some...", "Do you need any...?", "How much is...?" within their leaflet or in a short introductory/concluding sentence.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher encourages creativity in presentation (e.g., drawing pictures of items) but emphasizes clear and correct writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the completed informational leaflets. The assessment is formative, focusing on the students' ability to produce a written product that meets the learning outcome.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 5-7 target vocabulary items correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes prices for each item.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use "How much is...?", "some/any," or polite requests.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Text is legible and conveys clear information about the items.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic sentence structure is understandable (A1.3 level).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you wrote well on your leaflet?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one word you want to remember to spell correctly next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-6 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to write descriptions and prices for the farm stand items?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What would you add to your farm stand leaflet next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to create basic leaflets, demonstrating understanding of the task. The peer-checking activity was effective for minor corrections. Some students still struggle with distinguishing between "some" and "any" in their written output, which will require further reinforcement. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence frames for "some" and "any" during practice. The phonetic focus on /p/ and /b/ was subtly reinforced but could be made more active during writing. Students seemed engaged with creating their own farm stand advertisement.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their created leaflets to present their farm stands to classmates, integrating their writing with speaking and summarizing the information.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-4 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>